%I A400434 #40 Nov 14 2026 15:01:49
%S A400434 1,13,19,21,39,51,57,69,81,87,93,105,129,135,147,159,183,195,207,209,
%T A400434 221,227,251,257,269,293,305,311,335,347,359,383,389,401,425,449,467,
%U A400434 479,491,515,527,539,575,599,611,629,641,665,677,725,727,763,787,799,811,853,859,871
%N A400434 Sizes of the successive clusters of the hexagonal close-packed (HCP) lattice, centered on a lattice point.
%C A400434 The HCP structure is a non-Bravais lattice formed by an alternating ABAB... stacking of triangular A_2 sheets with an ideal height-to-base ratio c/L = sqrt(8/3).
%C A400434 Points in the HCP structure have Cartesian coordinates (x, y, z) = (L*(u + s + (v + t)/2), L*sqrt(3)*(v + t)/2, L*k*sqrt(2/3)), where u, v, k are integers, and L is the lattice parameter. Here (s, t) = (0, 0) for even k (A-layers), and (s, t) = (1/3, 1/3) for odd k (B-layers).
%C A400434 Scaling the squared Euclidean distance by 3 eliminates irrational axial spacing and yields a scaled squared distance metric D = 3*(R/L)^2. This reduces the boundary condition to the following ternary Diophantine inequalities for integer triples (u, v, k):
%C A400434 D = 3*(u^2 + u*v + v^2) + 2*k^2 <= 3*(R/L)^2 for even k;
%C A400434 D = 3*(u^2 + u*v + v^2 + u + v) + 1 + 2*k^2 <= 3*(R/L)^2 for odd k.
%C A400434 The shell counts at a given metric D can be expressed as a linear combination of four positive-definite integral ternary theta series. Let H(q) = Sum_{D>=0} N(D)*q^D be the generating function for the number of HCP sites N(D) at metric D. Let A(q) = Sum_{u,v in Z} q^(u^2 + u*v + v^2) and T(q) = Sum_{k in Z} q^(k^2). Then 2*H(q) = A(q)*T(q^2) - A(q^3)*T(q^2) - A(q)*T(q^8) + 3*A(q^3)*T(q^8). (This theta-series formulation was contributed by _Alper Ferudun_ derived from the enumeration script.)
%C A400434 This sequence gives the partial sums of the nonzero terms of the HCP theta series A004012, where the valid metrics D are cataloged in A005870 = {0, 3, 6, 8, 9, 11, 12, ...}.
%C A400434 While A007899 and A007202 give the coordination and crystal ball sequences of the HCP structure considered as an infinite regular graph (shortest graph-hop distance), A400434 gives the cluster populations within a continuous spatial Euclidean distance R.
%C A400434 Differs from the FCC cluster sequence A119869 starting at the third non-origin shell (index n = 3). For FCC (A119869), the initial terms are 1, 13, 19, 43, indicating shells of 12, 6, and 24 points. For HCP (A400434), the initial terms are 1, 13, 19, 21, where the third non-origin shell contains only 2 points due to the axial layer truncation.
%D A400434 J. H. Conway and N. J. A. Sloane, Sphere Packings, Lattices and Groups, Springer-Verlag, 3rd ed., 1999.
%H A400434 Andrew J. Morris, <a href="/A400434/b400434.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>.
%H A400434 Andrew J. Morris, <a href="/A400434/a400434.txt">C++ program for HCP Euclidean metric coordination</a>.
%F A400434 a(n) = Sum_{k=0..n} A401221(k).
%F A400434 a(n) = Sum_{k=0..n} A004012(A005870(k)).
%e A400434 The first few shells and cumulative cluster counts are:
%e A400434 n = 0: D = 0, R = 0, origin atom, a(0) = 1.
%e A400434 n = 1: D = 3, R = 1, 12 kissing spheres (6 in-plane at k=0, 3 in layer k=1, 3 in layer k=-1), cumulative a(1) = 1 + 12 = 13.
%e A400434 n = 2: D = 6, R = sqrt(2), 6 second neighbors (3 in layer k=1, 3 in layer k=-1), cumulative a(2) = 13 + 6 = 19.
%e A400434 n = 3: D = 8, R = sqrt(8/3), 2 axial neighbors at k=+-2, cumulative a(3) = 19 + 2 = 21.
%e A400434 n = 4: D = 9, R = sqrt(3), 18 neighbors (6 in-plane at k=0, 6 in layer k=1, 6 in layer k=-1), cumulative a(4) = 21 + 18 = 39.
%e A400434 n = 5: D = 11, R = sqrt(11/3), 12 neighbors (6 in layer k=2, 6 in layer k=-2), cumulative a(5) = 39 + 12 = 51.
%e A400434 n = 6: D = 12, R = 2, 6 in-plane neighbors at k=0, cumulative a(6) = 51 + 6 = 57.
%o A400434 (C++) // See Links.
%Y A400434 Cf. A004012, A004015, A005870, A007202, A007899, A119869, A401221.
%K A400434 nonn,easy,new
%O A400434 0,2
